{"id":"burning_barrel","compound_name":"Graphs::Aero::burning_barrel","source_path":"burning_barrel/burning_barrel.json","category":"Fire","author":"Autodesk","description":"An aero combustion simulation creating a burning barrel effect\n","documentation_file":"burning_barrel_docs_ENU.md","documentation":"# Burning Barrel\n\n### What it does\n\nCreates an aero combustion simulation for a burning barrel effect.\n\n### Inputs and Outputs\n\nThe input is the fire-emitting geometry — in this case, a bifrost sphere with added noise.  The output is the fire volume.\n\n### Useful information\n\n- To achieve a regular fire simulation, first turn off `combustion_expansion` on the `aero_combustion_settings` node (or set it to low values). Then, keep the input temperatures and the `burn_rate` low to let the natural heat produced from the combustion reaction drive the behavior. In this graph, the `ignition_temperature` is set to room temperature and the `burn_rate` is nearly zero. The source_air `temperature` is varied to create non-uniform burning intensities across the input geometry at ignition.\n\n- Since this is not an explosion or high-energy simulation and does not rely on heat produced to ignite another source, `oxygen_diffusion` and `radiative_heating` on the `aero_combustion_settings` node can be turned off without losing the realism of the simulation.  This significantly improves performance.\n\n- You can play with the burn rate and the value of cooling to make higher/lower & more/less energetic flames, and to produce different levels of smoke. Note that a custom `random_value` compound is used to randomly change the cooling during the simulation. This compound is also used to vary the temperature on the source.\n\n- Make sure to adjust the shader when changing the burn rate or the cooling.","library_dependencies":{"Amino":"2.15.0.9","bif":"2.15.0.9","fields":"2.15.0.9","fluids":"2.15.0.9","geometries":"2.15.0.9","simulation":"2.15.0.9"},"graph_ports":[{"portName":"f